==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|wizzan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*witaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*weyd-||see, know}}. Compare Dutch {{m|nl|weten}}, Danish {{m|da|vide}}, Swedish {{m|sv|veta}}, archaic Modern English verb {{m|en|wit}} and Latin {{m|la|videō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|DE-wissen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{audio|BY-wissen.ogg|Audio (Bavarian)|lang=de}}
* {{IPA|/ˈvɪsn̩/|/ˈvɪsən/|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-irregular|weiß|wusste|gewusst}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[know]]; to be [[aware]] of (a fact)
#: ''Ich '''weiß''', wo du bist.'' — “I know where you are.”
#: ''von etwas '''wissen''''' — “to know about something”

====Usage notes====
* See {{m|de|kennen}} for the distinction between this verb and ''wissen.''

